@meta
id: 24-16
title: Repeat the last command line
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: cmdline-edit
par: 28
minutes: 5
vscode: Alt-Up, Alt-Up, Alt-Up, Alt-Up
open: order-status.enum.ts

@brief
The enum members are supposed to follow the order lifecycle:
`PENDING`, `PAID`, `SHIPPED`, `DELIVERED`, `CANCELLED`, `REFUNDED`.
Somebody appended the first two to the bottom. `PENDING` on line 6
has to go to the top of the list, and `PAID` on line 7 directly below
it.

You know `:m-2`. Each of these lines needs it four times. `@:` repeats
the last command line, and like most things in normal mode it takes a
count: `3@:` runs it three more times. Type `:m-2` once per line and
let `@:` do the rest.

@keys
| `@:` | repeat the last command line |
| `3@:` | repeat it three times |
| `@@` | after `@:`, repeat it again |
| `:m-2` | the current line, one slot up |

@hints
The cursor follows the line as it moves, so a repeated `:m-2` keeps
bubbling the same line.
--
From line 6 to line 2 is four moves: one typed, three repeated. Same
for `PAID` once `PENDING` is out of its way.
--
`6G:m-2` Enter `3@:`, then `7G:m-2` Enter `3@:`, then `:wq`.

@solution
    6G :m-2<Enter> 3@:   7G :m-2<Enter> 3@:   :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes.

`@:` is the dot command for the command line. Anything you ran with a
colon, `@:` runs again, and a count in front runs it that many times.
It is why the relative form `:m-2` was worth learning over `:6m1`: a
command with no absolute numbers in it is safe to repeat from anywhere.
Bubbling a line up by watching it move and pressing `@:` until it
looks right is a better experience than counting the destination, and
it is not slower.

The same trick works for `:t.` to stamp out copies, `:j` to keep
joining, `:>` to keep indenting, and for a `:normal` sequence, which
is the chapter 25 way of running normal-mode keys from the command
line and is the other thing people put `@:` in front of.

@start order-status.enum.ts
export enum OrderStatus {
  SHIPPED = 'SHIPPED',
  DELIVERED = 'DELIVERED',
  CANCELLED = 'CANCELLED',
  REFUNDED = 'REFUNDED',
  PENDING = 'PENDING',
  PAID = 'PAID',
}

@expected order-status.enum.ts
export enum OrderStatus {
  PENDING = 'PENDING',
  PAID = 'PAID',
  SHIPPED = 'SHIPPED',
  DELIVERED = 'DELIVERED',
  CANCELLED = 'CANCELLED',
  REFUNDED = 'REFUNDED',
}
